[38;2;124;124;124mLet me first do the answer leak check. The problem text is:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m"Find the limit of $X_t$ as $t$ approaches 1 in the solution of the Brownian [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mbridge stochastic differential equation (SDE)."[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNo leaked answers, no solution sketches, no formalization notes. Clean problem.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow let me solve this.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Brownian bridge SDE is:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$dX_t = \frac{X_t}{t-1} dt + dW_t, \quad X_0 = 0$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mfor $t \in [0, 1)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a linear SDE. Let me solve it.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe general form of a linear SDE is:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$dX_t = (a(t)X_t + b(t))dt + (c(t)X_t + d(t))dW_t$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHere $a(t) = \frac{1}{t-1}$, $b(t) = 0$, $c(t) = 0$, $d(t) = 1$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe solution to a linear SDE with $c(t) = 0$ is:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$X_t = \Phi(t) \left( X_0 + \int_0^t \Phi(s)^{-1} b(s) ds + \int_0^t [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\Phi(s)^{-1} d(s) dW_s \right)$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mwhere $\Phi(t) = \exp\left(\int_0^t a(s) ds\right)$ is the fundamental solution.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mCompute $\Phi(t)$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\int_0^t \frac{1}{s-1} ds = \ln|t-1| - \ln|0-1| = \ln(1-t)$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m(since for $t < 1$, $|t-1| = 1-t$ and $|0-1| = 1$)[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $\Phi(t) = e^{\ln(1-t)} = 1-t$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAnd $\Phi(s)^{-1} = \frac{1}{1-s}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $b(t) = 0$ and $X_0 = 0$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$X_t = (1-t) \int_0^t \frac{1}{1-s} dW_s$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow I need to find $\lim_{t \to 1^-} X_t$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$$X_t = (1-t) \int_0^t \frac{1}{1-s} dW_s$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me define $M_t = \int_0^t \frac{1}{1-s} dW_s$. This is a martingale with [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mquadratic variation $\langle M \rangle_t = \int_0^t \frac{1}{(1-s)^2} ds$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAs $t \to 1^-$, $\int_0^t \frac{1}{(1-s)^2} ds = \frac{1}{1-t} - 1 \to \infty$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $M_t$ diverges (its quadratic variation goes to infinity).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe need to find the limit of $(1-t) M_t$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me compute the quadratic variation of $X_t$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\langle X \rangle_t = (1-t)^2 \int_0^t \frac{1}{(1-s)^2} ds = (1-t)^2 [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\left(\frac{1}{1-t} - 1\right) = (1-t) - (1-t)^2 = (1-t)(1 - (1-t)) = (1-t) [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\cdot t$$[0m

